#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Install Package File and Remove Apps, for EasySpeak-OS.

  easyspeak-os-packages install [FILE]   install an .rpm or .deb (no FILE: pick one from Downloads)
  easyspeak-os-packages remove           remove an app installed this way
  easyspeak-os-packages check FILE       say what a package needs, without installing anything
  easyspeak-os-packages repair           set a broken app box up again and put its apps back
                                         (--if-broken: only if one does not work; System Update uses this)
  easyspeak-os-packages login            choose box apps that start when you log in
  (easyspeak-os-packages after-restart runs at login, once, after something was added to the system)

The system itself is an image, so apps from .rpm and .deb files go into a box (a Distrobox container):
.rpm files into a Fedora box, .deb files into an Ubuntu box. Each box is made the first time it is needed,
and System Update keeps it current. Installed apps are added to Show Apps.

Before installing, the file is checked for parts a box cannot provide:
  - hardware rules (udev) that only set device permissions are copied onto the system, with the
    administrator password; rules that run programs are not;
  - drivers, firmware, printer and scanner drivers, login and network plugins and background services
    without an app need the system itself. An .rpm can be added to the system image instead (with the
    administrator password, after a restart). A .deb cannot.
If Flathub has the same app, that is offered first. A package that only adds a download source (a repository)
adds it to the box, then offers the apps from it.

What was installed is recorded in ~/.local/share/easyspeak-os/apps.json, so Remove Apps can undo it.
"""

